  <header class="flow">
    <h1>Best pollen and hay fever apps in Australia</h1>
    <p>We make one of these, so read this with that in mind. This is not a ranking with Respira at the top. It is what each app is genuinely best at, taken from its Australian App Store listing and its own website on {{ A.snapshotDate }}, including the places where another app is the better answer, and in Australia that is most of the big cities.</p>
  </header>

  <section class="section flow" aria-labelledby="short-answer">
    <h2 id="short-answer">The short answer</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Melbourne and Victoria:</strong> Melbourne Pollen Count. Counts from the state's pollen network, alerts for the thunderstorm asthma forecast, and more than 10,000 ratings.</li>
      <li><strong>Canberra, Sydney and Perth:</strong> the local apps from the same team (Canberra Pollen Count, Sydney Pollen Count &amp; Forecast, Perth Pollen Count), each fed by a real pollen trap in that city.</li>
      <li><strong>Tasmania, and bushfire smoke anywhere:</strong> AirRater.</li>
      <li><strong>Just the Victorian thunderstorm asthma forecast:</strong> the Thunderstorm Asthma app, or VicEmergency itself.</li>
      <li><strong>Air quality first:</strong> IQAir AirVisual.</li>
      <li><strong>One app for anywhere in Australia, and abroad:</strong> Respira. Grass and cypress pine from the Google Pollen API for any place in the country, a symptom diary, and the same app in 34 other countries. It uses a forecast model, not local traps, and has no Australian ratings yet.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

