    <h3>Respira</h3>
    <p class="small text-secondary">Pablo Fernández Jiménez · Anywhere in Australia, and 34 other countries · best for: one app wherever you are</p>
    <p>Respira reports grass and cypress pine (Callitris) for any place in Australia, from the Google Pollen API, updated daily, with the coming days, a map of 20 Australian cities and air quality for the same place. When there is no reading for a species, it says so instead of showing a zero. It keeps a symptom and medication diary; alerts at your own threshold, a trip planner and a PDF report for your GP are in Respira Pro. It does not offer a thunderstorm asthma forecast, and Google's forecast is a model, not a count from a local trap.</p>
    <p class="small text-secondary">{{ A.respira.price }}. {{ A.respira.rating }}. <a href="/en/pollen-australia">When the pollen season runs in Australia</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="section flow" aria-labelledby="thunderstorm">
    <h2 id="thunderstorm">Thunderstorm asthma</h2>
    <p>Epidemic thunderstorm asthma happens when there is a lot of grass pollen in the air and a particular kind of thunderstorm arrives, and many people develop asthma symptoms in a short time (<a href="https://www.betterhealth.vic.gov.au/health/conditionsandtreatments/thunderstorm-asthma" rel="noopener">Better Health Channel</a>). On 21 November 2016, Melbourne had the world's largest recorded event: it contributed to 10 deaths (<a href="https://www.health.vic.gov.au/environmental-health/epidemic-thunderstorm-asthma-program" rel="noopener">Victorian Department of Health</a>).</p>
    <p>Victoria publishes an official epidemic thunderstorm asthma risk forecast every day from 1 October to 31 December, three days ahead, on <a href="https://www.health.vic.gov.au/environmental-health/epidemic-thunderstorm-asthma-risk-forecast" rel="noopener">VicEmergency</a>. Melbourne Pollen Count and the Thunderstorm Asthma app pass it on; Respira does not. If you have asthma, follow the advice of your doctor and your asthma action plan.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="section flow" aria-labelledby="honest">
    <h2 id="honest">Where Respira is not the right answer</h2>
    <p>If you live in Melbourne, Canberra, Sydney or Perth, the local app reads a pollen trap in your city. Respira's Australian data is Google's forecast model: useful anywhere, but not a count.</p>
    <p>If thunderstorm asthma is your worry, use the Victorian forecast, through VicEmergency or one of the apps above. Respira does not carry it.</p>
    <p>And if a large local user base is what makes you trust an app, Respira does not have one in Australia: it added the country in September 2026 and has no Australian ratings yet.</p>
  </section>

